# Copy to .env.test (repo root, sibling of .env) for running services/api's
# e2e test layer locally. Never commit the real .env.test file — same rule
# as .env, see .gitignore.
#
# Lives at the repo root, not services/api/, mirroring .env.example's own
# location — CLAUDE.md's "one root .env for now" reasoning (a single
# backend-only env file, not one per workspace) applies just as much to the
# test variant: services/api/test/env.ts is the only thing that reads this
# file, but there's no benefit to giving it a different home than the file
# it's a sibling/variant of.
#
# CORRECTED framing (PR #59 follow-up fix — see services/api/test/README.md
# for the full incident writeup): this file was originally written as "only
# DATABASE_URL needs a test-specific value, everything else is safe to
# share with local dev." That was true by accident, not by design — it
# described what happened to work on a machine that already had a local
# .env with a real JWT_SECRET in it, not what the e2e layer actually
# requires to run standalone. CI has no such local .env, and PR #59's own
# first real CI run proved it: `JwtModule.registerAsync` in
# auth-foundation.module.ts reads JWT_SECRET with no code-level default, so
# an unset value only surfaces once a real jwtService.sign() call runs —
# which a genuinely bootstrapped e2e AppModule does, and which the
# previously mocked-Prisma-only unit suite never did.
#
# The corrected rule: DATABASE_URL needs a test-specific *value* (a
# separate database, for isolation — see test/README.md); JWT_SECRET needs
# to be present here *at all*, because it's a required config key with no
# fallback and nothing else in this repo guarantees it's set in every
# environment that runs the e2e suite. Every other config key the app reads
# (REDIS_URL, EMAIL_PROVIDER_API_KEY, SENTRY_DSN, the various *_TTL_*
# overrides, etc.) genuinely is safe to omit here — each of those either
# has a code-level default or degrades to an explicit, logged "not
# configured" no-op (see services/api/src/instrument.ts and
# registration-email.service.ts/password-reset-email.service.ts for that
# pattern). JWT_SECRET is the one exception: signing/verification is never
# allowed to silently fall back to an empty or default secret (that would
# be a real security regression, not a test convenience), so a working
# value has to come from configuration instead. See
# services/api/test/global-setup.ts for how the test database itself is
# created and migrated automatically on first use.

# --- Database (matches docker-compose.yml's local Postgres exactly, only
# the database name differs: "soccernity_test", not "soccernity" — so e2e
# runs never touch real local dev data) ---
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/soccernity_test"

# --- Auth ---
# Required — JwtModule.registerAsync (auth-foundation.module.ts) has no
# fallback if this is unset, and a real e2e AppModule bootstrap eventually
# calls jwtService.sign()/.verify() for real. Any obviously-fake value
# works for tests; it must never match a real deployment's JWT_SECRET (see
# .env.example's own comment on generating a real one for actual
# deployments).
JWT_SECRET="e2e-test-only-jwt-secret-do-not-use-in-any-real-environment"
